     "a": "The rules to determine the “truth” of any value which is not already of the Boolean type are:\n If the value is a number, it is false if exactly equal to zero and true otherwise.\n If the value is a string, it is false if the string is empty (has zero characters) or is the string “0”, and is\ntrue otherwise.\n Values of type NULL are always false.\n If the value is an array, it is false if it contains no other values, and it is true otherwise. For an object,\ncontaining a value means having a member variable that has been assigned a value.\n Valid resources are true (although some functions that return resources when they are successful\nwill return FALSE when unsuccessful).\n Don’t use double as Booleans."
